COMPONENT FAILURE MODES (FIELD-RELEVANT)
============================================================

COMPRESSOR FAILURES
-------------------
• Locked rotor → click-hum cycling, no pressure differential.
• Weak compression → warm suction, low ΔT across condenser.
• Open windings → dead compressor.
• Overheating → high amps, hot shell.

CONDENSER FAILURES
------------------
• Dirty coil → high head pressure, poor cooling.
• Failed condenser fan → compressor overheating, slow cabinet cooling.

EVAPORATOR FAILURES
-------------------
• Frost blockage → airflow collapse, warm fridge.
• Failed evaporator fan → freezer OK, fridge warm.
• Uneven frost → sealed-system issue.

CAPILLARY / FILTER-DRIER FAILURES
---------------------------------
• Restriction → low suction, cold cap tube inlet, partial frost pattern.
• Moisture freeze → intermittent cooling.

THERMOSTAT / SENSOR FAILURES
-----------------------------
• Stuck closed → compressor runs continuously.
• Stuck open → compressor never starts.
• Thermistor drift → erratic cycling.

DEFROST SYSTEM FAILURES
------------------------
• Failed heater/timer/sensor → evaporator encased in ice, airflow collapse.

START DEVICE FAILURES
----------------------
• Relay/overload → intermittent or no compressor start.

DIAGNOSTIC PRESSURE/TEMPERATURE MAPPING
============================================================

SUCTION PRESSURE (Low Side)
---------------------------
• Too high → weak compressor, high load, overcharge.
• Too low → restriction, undercharge, low load.

DISCHARGE PRESSURE (High Side)
------------------------------
• Too high → dirty condenser, failed fan, overcharge.
• Too low → undercharge, weak compressor.

LINE TEMPERATURES
-----------------
• Suction line: cool, not frosty (unless low charge/restriction).
• Discharge line: hot; warm = compressor not doing work.

EVAPORATOR FROST PATTERN
------------------------
• Full, even → healthy.
• Partial → sealed-system issue.
• Solid block → defrost failure.

TECHNICIAN WORKFLOW 
============================================================

1. Verify power + control board call for cool.
2. Check condenser coil + fan (fastest fix).
3. Evaluate compressor behavior (amps, shell temp, start device).
4. Inspect evaporator airflow (fan, frost pattern, damper).
5. Test thermistors (resistance vs. temp chart).
6. Only then consider sealed-system work (charge, restriction, compressor).

VAPOR-COMPRESSION REFRIGERATION CYCLE
============================================================

STANDARD NOTATION
-----------------
h   = specific enthalpy (kJ/kg)
s   = specific entropy (kJ/kg·K)
P   = pressure (kPa or bar)
T   = absolute temperature (K)
ṁ  = mass flow rate (kg/s)
q   = specific heat transfer (kJ/kg)
w   = specific work (kJ/kg)
Q̇  = heat transfer rate (kW)
Ẇ  = power (kW)
COP = coefficient of performance

State points:
1 = compressor inlet (evaporator outlet)
2 = compressor outlet (condenser inlet)
3 = condenser outlet (expansion device inlet)
4 = expansion device outlet (evaporator inlet)

1. COMPRESSOR WORK
============================================================

Specific compressor work:
    w_c = h_2 - h_1

Isentropic relation:
    s_2s = s_1
    h_2s = h(P_2, s_1)

Actual compressor work with isentropic efficiency η_is:
    w_c = (h_2s - h_1) / η_is
or
    h_2 = h_1 + (h_2s - h_1)/η_is

2. CONDENSER HEAT REJECTION
============================================================

Specific heat rejected:
    q_H = h_2 - h_3

3. EXPANSION PROCESS
============================================================

Isenthalpic throttling (capillary tube):
    h_4 = h_3

4. EVAPORATOR REFRIGERATION EFFECT
============================================================

Specific cooling:
    q_L = h_1 - h_4
        = h_1 - h_3

5. COEFFICIENT OF PERFORMANCE
============================================================

COP:
    COP = q_L / w_c
        = (h_1 - h_4) / (h_2 - h_1)
        = (h_1 - h_3) / (h_2 - h_1)

Carnot limit:
    COP_Carnot = T_L / (T_H - T_L)

COP\text{COP}\text{COP}
 = coefficient of performance (dimensionless)  

Subscripts:  ( 1 ) = compressor inlet (evaporator outlet)  
( 2 ) = compressor outlet (condenser inlet)  
( 3 ) = condenser outlet (expansion device inlet)  
( 4 ) = expansion device outlet (evaporator inlet)  
( L ) = low-temperature (evaporator / refrigerated space)  
( H ) = high-temperature (condenser / ambient)  
( c ) = compressor  
( is ) = isentropic

6. RATE EQUATIONS
============================================================

Cooling capacity:
    Q̇_L = ṁ * q_L
         = ṁ (h_1 - h_4)

Compressor power:
    Ẇ_c = ṁ * w_c
         = ṁ (h_2 - h_1)

Condenser heat rejection:
    Q̇_H = ṁ (h_2 - h_3)
         = Q̇_L + Ẇ_c

7. MASS FLOW & PRESSURE RELATIONS
============================================================

Mass flow from cooling load:
    ṁ = Q̇_L / (h_1 - h_4)

Pressure ratio:
    r_p = P_2 / P_1
        = P_cond / P_evap

8. COMPRESSOR VOLUMETRIC EFFICIENCY
============================================================

Approximate volumetric efficiency:
    η_v = 1 - C[(P_2/P_1)^(1/γ) - 1]

C = clearance ratio
γ = specific heat ratio of refrigerant vapor

9. SATURATION RELATION
============================================================

For any refrigerant (e.g., R600a):
    P_sat ↔ T_sat via refrigerant property tables/software.

No simple closed-form equation; use REFPROP or tables.

SUPERHEATING & SUBCOOLING 
============================================================

STATE POINTS (REFERENCE)
------------------------
1 = Compressor inlet (evaporator outlet)
2 = Compressor outlet (condenser inlet)
3 = Condenser outlet (expansion inlet)
4 = Expansion outlet (evaporator inlet)

. SUPERHEATING
============================================================

DEFINITION
----------
Superheating = vapor temperature above saturation temperature
at evaporator (suction) pressure.

Formula:
    ΔT_sh = T_1 - T_sat(P_1)

Where:
    T_1       = actual suction-line temperature
    T_sat(P1) = saturation temperature at suction pressure

WHERE IT OCCURS
---------------
• Inside evaporator (useful superheat)
• Suction line (additional superheat)

ASCII DIAGRAM (EVAPORATOR REGION)
---------------------------------
Saturated vapor →→→ Superheated vapor

    |<---- saturation line ---->|<-- superheat -->|
    h_sat(P1)                   h_1

EFFECTS — POSITIVE
------------------
• Ensures dry vapor enters compressor.
• Prevents liquid slugging.
• Provides margin for load variation.

EFFECTS — NEGATIVE
------------------
• h_1 increases.
• Refrigeration effect q_L = h_1 - h_4 increases slightly.
• Compressor work w_c = h_2 - h_1 increases.
• COP usually decreases or stays neutral.
• Discharge temperature rises.
• Higher specific volume → lower ṁ for fixed displacement → lower capacity.

TYPICAL VALUES
--------------
Domestic/light commercial: 5–10 K (9–18 °F)

2. SUBCOOLING
============================================================

DEFINITION
----------
Subcooling = liquid temperature below saturation temperature
at condenser (discharge) pressure.

Formula:
    ΔT_sc = T_sat(P_3) - T_3

Where:
    T_3       = actual liquid-line temperature
    T_sat(P3) = saturation temperature at condenser pressure

WHERE IT OCCURS
---------------
• End of condenser coil
• Liquid-line subcooler
• Suction–liquid heat exchanger (some systems)

ASCII DIAGRAM (CONDENSER REGION)
--------------------------------
Saturated liquid ←←← Subcooled liquid

    |<-- subcooling -->|<---- saturation line ---->|
    h_3                h_sat(P3)

EFFECTS — POSITIVE
------------------
• h_3 decreases.
• Expansion is isenthalpic → h_4 = h_3.
• Refrigeration effect increases:
      q_L = h_1 - h_4 = h_1 - h_3
• COP increases:
      COP = (h_1 - h_3) / (h_2 - h_1)
• Less flash gas → better evaporator utilization.
• Ensures pure liquid feed to expansion device.

EFFECTS — NEGATIVE
------------------
• Very few; excessive subcooling may require larger condenser or more fan power.

TYPICAL VALUES
--------------
5–15 K (9–27 °F)

3. TABLE
============================================================

| Parameter                  | Superheating                     | Subcooling                         |
|----------------------------|----------------------------------|------------------------------------|
| Refrigeration effect q_L   | Slight increase                  | Clear increase                     |
| Compressor work w_c        | Increases                        | Essentially unchanged              |
| COP                        | Slight decrease / neutral        | Increases                          |
| Cooling capacity           | Often decreases (lower ṁ)        | Increases                          |
| Compressor protection      | Essential                        | Indirect (better liquid feed)      |
| Discharge temperature      | Increases                        | Little direct effect               |

